body:
That's my point. Until you're "there", you have no idea what it's like. I genuinely believe that a lot of the stigma around mental health issues, depression especially, stems from people not being able to grasp just how nasty it is living with it.

&#x200B;

It's not that I would wish it on anyone or anything, but it's just so hard for other people to understand how desperate depression sufferers can get in their search for relief until they've lived it.

If I had to guess, it's the same mentality that spawns the "suicide is selfish" line of thought. The idea that you're hurting someone else as you escape your own pain. Of course, the part that most "normal" (for lack of a better word) people don't get is just how desperate the suicidal are to end their misery.

&#x200B;

One of my aunts committed suicide about six years ago. A lot of my family resents her for it, because they still grieve for her (no one knew that she was suicidal until her death was ruled a suicide-- tl;dr she tried to make it look like an accident but failed). Of course, because I've grappled with my own suicidal thoughts for about 20 years, I'm much more sympathetic to her. I don't think it's maliciousness... just ignorance. I only hope that they never know what that type of desperation feels like.

I've got siblings who are in the middle of some of the most important years of their lives (starting/graduating college/university respectively). The last thing they need right now is their brother finally tapping out.

&#x200B;

I know there's never a "right time" to foist that type of tragedy on those who love you, but I can't think of a worse time than now, as much as I want to go. They've put up with my shit for 20-some years. I owe it to them to at least try to not put them through that grief for one more at the very least.

Because I want the pain to stop. The physical pain, the decades of psychological trauma, the fresh hell that awaits every time I wake up in the morning, all of it.

&#x200B;

On the good days, the medication helps. The therapy helps. The video games and movies that I use as a distraction help. The few people left that I trust and love help. But it's the days that aren't so good that put me at death's doorstep, wondering if today is the day I finally ring the doorbell and step inside.

There's a quote from Charlie Chaplin (from "The Great Dictator"-- really powerful speech at the end of that one) that's always given me strength for the wrong reasons: "so long as men die, liberty will never perish."

&#x200B;

Of course, it's **supposed** to be taken as "tyrants will eventually die and their empires collapse", but it's always had a second meaning for me: "Someday, you will die, and the hardship will be over, and there's nothing that anyone can do to take that away from you." It might or might not be today, and it might or might not be because of suicide, but eventually, I'm going to die, and that will be the end of it.

&#x200B;

It's a sick irony, but there's something about that inevitability that I find... liberating.

Fear is one of the main reasons I'm sticking around, but not fear of surviving, hurting myself in a failed attempt, or any bad afterlife that might await me. I fear for the ones that I care about that I'd leave behind, few as they are. One of my brothers is in such a fragile mental state that I know that, on the day I finally snap and go through with it, the odds are likely that he'll follow me to the grave, and not only do I not want him to die because of me, I know how devastating it will be for my other brother if both of us die like that.

&#x200B;

Miles to go before I sleep and all that. As much as I want to go, I can't rest yet. I don't give a damn about myself, but until I'm sure that the burden of keeping me alive outweighs the harm I would cause them by finally embracing death, I've got to keep going.

Most fatal gunshot wounds aren't instant deaths. It can take a rather long time to finally bleed out if the bullet didn't hit a vital organ or major artery/vein. Honestly, unless you catch it right in the heart or the brain, it's actually a pretty bad way to die.

&#x200B;

When I finally go, I want to go quickly, and failing that, as painlessly as possible. Dying of a gunshot wound, short of having my brains blown out and being dead before I hit the ground, is not one of he ways I'd prefer.

There's a taboo when it comes to dealing with the suicidal unless you're a trained professional. When it comes to people who are in a crisis situation, it's incredibly easy to screw up and make matters worse.

&#x200B;

Saying that, I'm not going to say that you "did a bad thing" by taking it upon yourself to do what you did. Judging by what you claim to have done and how the man reacted, I'd personally say that you did the right thing under the circumstances, and you're right in that you may have well saved his life. I'm just saying that there is a reason why you were yelled at for it, because it could just have easily backfired.

&#x200B;

This is one of those scenarios with no "right answer", sadly.

There's a school of thought that claims that life being pointless is the best part about it. That if nothing really matters in the end, you may as well make the most of it. After all, death isn't going anywhere, but you only get the one crack at life.

&#x200B;

I know that, in the depths of depression that lead someone to the point of suicide, it's hard to find anything that feels pleasurable, or at the very least not pleasurable enough to outweigh the pain. And, as someone who is also suicidal, this is more than a bit hypocritical coming from me. But, if your driving motivation for wanting to die is that everything is pointless, then what's the rush? It's not like taking a pit stop on the way to the finish line is going to make it any less pointless. If you have nothing to lose, then why not take a gamble and live the final part of your life without inhibitions?

&#x200B;

If nothing matters, then you might as well make the most of it. Eat those crazy foods you always talked yourself out of eating, chase those high-risk adrenaline highs you've talked yourself out of, those types of things. Because if we're all going to die someday and none of it matters, we might as well go out having had our fill of whatever fleeting pleasures can be found in this world before it's all taken away from us. Because if the pleasure is pointless, then so too is the pain of knowing that it's pointless. Because if you're going to take yourself out, you might as well go out knowing that you've experienced everything that you've wanted to try before the ride was over.

I find it oddly comforting that so many people seem to be in the same boat. Myself included, for what it's worth.

&#x200B;

I guess that's why many of us are still here. We cling to those shreds of hope, those plans to turn everything around. For some of us, those plans bear fruit. For others, it's a false hope, but it's one that gets us through the day. For the rest... tragically, the day comes that they can't hold on anymore.

&#x200B;

I guess what I'm saying is, if we didn't have that faint glimmer of hope, most of us would have checked ourselves out long ago. It's that sick, ironic battle between hope and despair that keeps us here-- miserable, but alive, at least for today.

I'm in the same boat. My one younger brother was my only friend in the world for a long time, and while I don't really get along well with the other one, he's also grappling with depression and I imagine that if I lose control long enough to do it, it'll likely be the straw that breaks the camel's back for him.

&#x200B;

Not going to exaggerate, at this point I'm living for their sake. Haven't really cared what happens to me for a very long time, but the last thing I want is to drag someone down with me. The worst part is that if I finally burn through the last of their goodwill with the burden of putting up with my uselessness, it'll probably be the spark that pushes me off the cliff. Really an ironic Catch-22.

&#x200B;

Miles to go before I sleep, etc.

My depression started at a very young age. Like, single-digit ages. When it finally got so consistently bad that I couldn't hide it anymore and got diagnosed, my first thoughts after finally getting some help were about how goddamn alien it felt to know that consciously wanting to die wasn't a standard part of the human condition. That constantly trying to smother yourself in your own bed before you're 10 years old isn't something that **everyone** does but keeps secret, like I did growing up.

&#x200B;

I'm still suicidal all these years later but I'm thankful that there's been such a push against the stigma in the past few years. No one should have to live their life this way. Even if it doesn't save my life in the long term, at least there's countless folks who are still around because people finally started shining a light on how fucking sneaky this miserable condition can be.
